In ordinary Moth code, `--` starts a single-line comment.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- Explain why this value exists.
    retry_count = 3
]

Everything after `--` on that source-code line is comment text.

### Template and moth template text

Template bodies are content, not ordinary code-token context.

Inside a template body, `--` is ordinary body text. A moth template `.mtf` file is
the body of an implicit compile-time Markdown template, so `--` is text there
too.

Use [$html:<code>&#91;$note: ...&#93;</code>] to discard template-authored comment content. `$todo`
annotates the next source statement or declaration and is not a template
comment. `$doc` likewise annotates declarations. See @../directives (Directives).

### Naming conventions

- Variables and functions use `regular_snake_case`.
- Structs, choices, type aliases and generic parameters use `PascalCase`.
- Trait names use all-caps identifiers such as `DISPLAY_TEXT`.
- Names should describe their role rather than their storage or implementation detail.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    retry_count = 3

    load_profile |user_id String| -> String:
        return "profile"
    ;

    UserProfile = |
        display_name String,
    |
]

### Name collisions

A visible name cannot be redeclared while it remains in scope.

The complete no-shadowing contract is documented by
@../bindings/#shadowing (Values and bindings). Dependency aliases and module API
names have additional collision rules in the Packages documentation.

### Identifier validity and reserved names

Identifiers start with `_` or a Unicode alphabetic character and continue with
`_` or a Unicode alphanumeric character. Exact source-word recognition remains
case-sensitive, including `this` and `This`. `moth-lexical` owns one shared
source-word inventory and user-name predicate for Moth and MON, including
reserved-only names. The predicate strips leading underscores and
matches reserved words without regard to ASCII case. It applies no Unicode
normalisation or XID substitution. Naming conventions above describe style, not
lexical validity: a style mismatch can produce a warning without making an
otherwise valid identifier invalid.

The shared policy reserves the numeric names `I8`, `I16`, `I32`, `I64`, `U8`,
`U16`, `U32`, `U64`, `F16`, `F32`, `F64` and `Byte`. It also reserves the Dec
family: bare `dec` and any ASCII-digit suffix match after leading underscores
are removed, without regard to case. This includes invalid type spellings such
as `dec01` and `dec257`. `DecBox` remains an ordinary name.

Type recognition stays stricter: only canonical `Dec` and `Dec0` through
`Dec256` spellings name Dec types. The removed `Int`, `Uint` and `Float`
spellings receive no builtin alias or special reservation. `uint` and
`UintBox` follow ordinary identifier rules.

Any reservation change alters which MON documents and schemas pass, so it is a
MON format compatibility change. Such a change updates the shared rule, the
cross-parser conformance tests and the release-facing documentation together.
See @../mon/#mon-format (MON format).

The former `Number` spellings are not reserved. `Number`, `number`, `Number2`
and `NumberBox` are ordinary user names again, and `Number`-family spellings no
longer name builtin types. Without a matching user declaration, `Number` or
`Number2` in a type position reports an unknown-type diagnostic. There is no
legacy `Number` type alias.

### Configuration names

Source directives use the `$` marker. `$export` marks public declarations and direct-selection re-exports in `@mod.moth` and `@package.moth`. `$config` marks typed build inputs, `$doc` records declaration documentation and `$todo` annotates source statements or declarations. They can share one line or occupy consecutive lines before the same target, but duplicate names are invalid. Project and active-builder settings remain ordinary named records, so `$project`, `$builder` and `$html_builder` are not directives.

Directive spellings do not reserve ordinary identifiers. Names such as `export`, `project`, `html_builder` and `entry` are available outside directive syntax.
The file `config.moth` and the dependency root `@project` retain their separate
path and package semantics.

The shared identifier rules reserve `Config`, including case-insensitive spellings
and leading-underscore variants. This also applies to MON names.

`import` is not a keyword. `Import` is an ordinary valid PascalCase user name:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    Import = |
        source String,
    |
]

Ordinary dependency clauses do not use an `import` keyword.

`block`, `group` and `region` are not keywords. They follow ordinary identifier policy. In executable statement position, each corresponding `name:` header has the same reserved declared-region shape as any other valid identifier.
